      { "input": "abc", "hash": "0x44bc2cf5ad770999" }
    ],
    "_asciiRowsREADME": [
      "The three short ASCII XXH64 vectors that circulate in almost every port of xxHash",
      "(seed 0, the raw UTF-8 bytes, no length prefix -- these are NOT canonical-encoding",
      "rows). The empty-input row is the same fact as the length-0 row above, restated in",
      "the form people actually recognise. All three were reproduced by",
      "System.IO.Hashing.XxHash64 before being written here."
    ]
  },

  "canonicalEncodingVectors": {
    "_README": [
      "Hash64 over the canonical byte encoding of 14 section 8.0:",
      "",
      "  ulong / long / int / enum  ->  widened to 64 bits (ints and signed enums",
      "                                 SIGN-extended), written as 8 bytes little-endian",
      "  string                     ->  a 4-byte little-endian UTF-8 BYTE count,",
      "                                 then the UTF-8 bytes",
      "",
      "Arguments are concatenated in argument order and the whole buffer is hashed once.",
      "",
      "`encodedByteLength` is the length of that buffer. It is asserted too: it catches an",
      "encoder that produces the right hash for the wrong reason, and it is the quickest",
      "way to see why a row moved.",
      "",
      "Enum rows name a test enum nested in ReferenceEnums (Rng/ReferenceVectors.cs), one",
      "per underlying integral type, so the widening rule is pinned for all eight.",
      "",
      "Some rows deliberately share a hash -- int -1, long -1 and ulong.MaxValue all encode",
      "to the same eight bytes. That is the rule working: the encoding is by value, not by",
      "declared type."
    ],
    "rows": [
